  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Few showers/storms today mainly south of the\nVA/NC border.\n\nA few showers still linger across the Blue Ridge northwest of\nLynchburg this morning associated with some upper divergence and\nlow level convergence with a trough/front in place. \n\nOverall, models show more sinking/subsidence kicking in behind\nthe upper trough today, with better support for storms shifting\nfurther south into the Carolinas/Georgia. Cannot rule out some\ndifferential heating with low level convergence showers/storms\ntoday mainly across our NC counties, but trends favor most of\nthe area staying dry. Pops have been reduced to 20-30 percent at\nmost from the Blue Ridge of VA south of Roanoke/Blacksburg into\nNC. Not anticipating any severe or flooding concerns.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Heat returns next week.\n\nA expanding 5h ridge will shift east into our area early next\nweek suppressing convection. Temperatures soar to as much as 10\ndegrees above normal with 90s returning Monday and lasting \nthrough at least Thursday for the foothills/Piedmont including\nRoanoke, even some 90s possible in the mountain valleys. By \nlate next week trough sinks further south into the northern \nMid- Atlantic and could start to see convection ramp up again.",
